{
  "rules": [
    "Apply a persistent downward bias correction: The model consistently overestimates prices by 5-10 EUR, particularly failing to anticipate the sharp structural drop from ~64 EUR to the 52-58 EUR range observed in late January/early February.",
    "Correct for excessive mean reversion: The model forecasts a slow, linear recovery or stability, whereas true outcomes exhibit high volatility with rapid drops followed by partial rebounds; adjust forecasts to reflect steeper initial declines and sharper, non-linear recoveries.",
    "Invert sentiment weighting: High sentiment scores (1.00) in the history correlate with subsequent price drops in the dataset; treat bullish sentiment signals as contrarian indicators for immediate short-term price action.",
    "Reduce forecast horizon lag: The model reacts too slowly to the established downtrend; accelerate the decay rate in the forecast curve to align with the actual velocity of the price collapse seen in the first 10 days of the outcome window.",
    "Adjust for overshoot after volatility: When history shows a spike (e.g., 65.80) followed by a drop, the model fails to price in the subsequent lower floor; lower the baseline forecast by 3-4 EUR below the immediate historical average to account for the new lower trading range."
  ]
}
